      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 9.2‑acre Kings Park itself hosts tennis courts, a playground, and picnic areas, a legacy of that original donation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kings_park_virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Long before settlers arrived, the area served as a Native American quartz quarry used for tool making over 13,000 years ago.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kings_park_virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Accotink Creek borders Kings Park to the east, and a small tributary flows through its northern part, feeding the larger watershed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/kings_park_virginia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Kings Park sits just 17 miles southwest of downtown Washington, D.C., and a few miles west of the Capital Beltway.",
